# MonKeys Project Rules & Instructions

Whenever you update the application code, you must compile, commit, and deploy the changes using the custom deploy script.

## 🛠️ Build Commands
* Run `./build.sh` to compile the app bundle. This builds:
  `build/MonKeys.app` (macOS 13.0+ target, arm64 only, Swift 6 compiler).
* The build output is located at: `build/MonKeys.app`.

## 🚀 Deployment Instructions
* Use the custom command `gdeploy` in the zsh terminal to commit, tag, and publish releases to GitHub.
* Usage:
  `gdeploy -prod "Commit message description" "v1.X.X"`
* This command will automatically:
  1. Compile the app bundle using `./build.sh`.
  2. Create a compressed package `build/MonKeys.zip` of the app.
  3. Commit all changed files to git.
  4. Push the branch to the private GitHub repository (`SafetH16/Monkeys`).
  5. Delete any old tag/release for the version if it exists, tag the commit, and push the tag.
  6. Create a GitHub Release for that tag, and upload `MonKeys.zip` as a release asset.

## ⚠️ Important Guidelines
* **Do NOT commit credentials:** The updater token lives ONLY in the untracked `.updater_token` file; `build.sh` injects it into the generated, gitignored `Monkeys/UpdaterToken.swift`. Never hardcode tokens in Swift sources or commit them — anything compiled in can be extracted with `strings`, and anything committed stays in git history forever.
* **One visible app:** the app is installed in `/Applications/MonKeys.app`. After installing an update, delete `build/MonKeys.app` so Spotlight/Launchpad show a single copy. Every reinstall invalidates the Accessibility grant (ad-hoc signature): re-grant it in System Settings.
* **Keep Swift 6 Concurrency:** Ensure all code remains strict-concurrency safe. AppDelegate is `@MainActor` isolated. All background work or nonisolated timers (e.g. `Timer.scheduledTimer`) must delegate UI tasks using `Task { @MainActor [weak self] in ... }` blocks.
* **Release Asset Naming:** The release asset name must always be `MonKeys.zip` for the auto-updater to work.
